WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:05.660
Picture the bank's busiest account on payday.

2
00:00:05.660 --> 00:00:10.212
Eight tellers, each one a thread, add deposits to its balance at once.

3
00:00:10.212 --> 00:00:15.934
Back in the lesson on count++, a plain long lost more than two thirds of those deposits.

4
00:00:15.934 --> 00:00:19.251
An atomic number fixed it, and we took it on trust.

5
00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:23.142
Last time, every lock made threads wait in line for a key.

6
00:00:23.142 --> 00:00:25.013
An atomic has no line at all.

7
00:00:25.013 --> 00:00:29.852
So how can two threads change one number safely, if neither one ever waits?

8
00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:32.648
Let's find out with a small job.

9
00:00:32.648 --> 00:00:36.679
The fraud team watches a screen with the day's largest payment.

10
00:00:36.679 --> 00:00:42.247
Many threads report payments, and each one raises the maximum if its payment is bigger.

11
00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:44.061
The first try is one line.

12
00:00:44.061 --> 00:00:47.320
If the amount is bigger than the maximum, write it.

13
00:00:47.467 --> 00:00:49.110
Let's slow it down.

14
00:00:49.110 --> 00:00:50.753
The maximum is 500.

15
00:00:50.753 --> 00:00:56.548
Thread 1 reports 900, and thread 2 reports 700, at the same moment.

16
00:00:56.548 --> 00:00:57.759
Both read 500.

17
00:00:57.759 --> 00:00:59.834
Both decide to raise it.

18
00:00:59.834 --> 00:01:01.564
Thread 1 writes 900.

19
00:01:01.564 --> 00:01:05.369
A moment later, thread 2 writes 700 over it.

20
00:01:05.533 --> 00:01:08.870
The day's biggest payment just vanished from the screen.

21
00:01:08.870 --> 00:01:13.397
And since max is volatile, every thread now sees the wrong answer, promptly.

22
00:01:13.533 --> 00:01:15.323
The trouble is the last step.

23
00:01:15.323 --> 00:01:16.742
Thread 2 wrote blindly.

24
00:01:16.742 --> 00:01:20.382
It never checked that the number was still the one it read.

25
00:01:20.382 --> 00:01:24.824
What we want is a careful write: change it only if nobody beat me to it.

26
00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:30.143
Picture a clerk who keeps a tally on a board behind the bank's counter.

27
00:01:30.143 --> 00:01:34.303
You may ask her to change it, but you must say what it shows now.

28
00:01:34.303 --> 00:01:36.223
It says 41, please make it 42.

29
00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:39.209
If the board still says 41, she writes 42.

30
00:01:39.209 --> 00:01:44.759
If someone got there first and it already says 42, she refuses, and you look again.

31
00:01:44.759 --> 00:01:47.300
She never makes anyone wait in a line.

32
00:01:47.467 --> 00:01:48.312
Let's map it.

33
00:01:48.312 --> 00:01:50.003
The board is the variable.

34
00:01:50.003 --> 00:01:52.864
What you say it shows is the expected value.

35
00:01:52.864 --> 00:01:55.336
The number you want is the next value.

36
00:01:55.336 --> 00:02:01.644
And the clerk is one processor instruction, so nobody can slip in between her look and her write.

37
00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:04.398
Java calls the request compareAndSet.

38
00:02:04.398 --> 00:02:09.244
If the value still equals expected, it stores next, and returns true.

39
00:02:09.244 --> 00:02:12.474
If not, it changes nothing, and returns false.

40
00:02:12.474 --> 00:02:14.861
Compare and set, or CAS for short.

41
00:02:15.533 --> 00:02:20.013
Now two threads add one to the tally at the same moment.

42
00:02:20.013 --> 00:02:21.052
Both read 41.

43
00:02:21.052 --> 00:02:24.492
Thread 1 asks first: 41, please make it 42.

44
00:02:24.492 --> 00:02:24.892
Done.

45
00:02:24.892 --> 00:02:28.011
Then thread 2 asks the very same thing.

46
00:02:28.011 --> 00:02:29.931
What does the clerk say?

47
00:02:32.933 --> 00:02:33.236
No.

48
00:02:33.236 --> 00:02:37.875
The board says 42 now, so thread 2 gets false.

49
00:02:37.875 --> 00:02:45.036
It drops its old plan, reads 42, and asks again: 42, please make it 43.

50
00:02:45.036 --> 00:02:45.440
Yes.

51
00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:46.549
41, 42, 43.

52
00:02:46.549 --> 00:02:48.163
Nothing is lost.

53
00:02:48.933 --> 00:02:51.818
That's the whole trick, written as a loop.

54
00:02:51.818 --> 00:02:52.848
Read the value.

55
00:02:52.848 --> 00:02:54.290
Decide what you want.

56
00:02:54.290 --> 00:02:55.251
Ask the clerk.

57
00:02:55.251 --> 00:03:01.020
If she says no, your decision was based on old news, so read again and decide again.

58
00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:03.238
Back to the fraud screen.

59
00:03:03.238 --> 00:03:07.559
Thread 2 read 500, and asks: 500, please make it 700.

60
00:03:07.559 --> 00:03:11.147
But the board says 900, so the answer is no.

61
00:03:11.147 --> 00:03:16.202
It reads 900, sees that 700 isn't bigger, and leaves it alone.

62
00:03:16.202 --> 00:03:18.321
The biggest payment stays.

63
00:03:19.067 --> 00:03:21.302
Here it is in Java and in Kotlin.

64
00:03:21.302 --> 00:03:25.773
Read, decide, and write only if the maximum is still what we read.

65
00:03:25.773 --> 00:03:27.535
Otherwise, go round again.

66
00:03:28.333 --> 00:03:30.479
How often does the clerk say no?

67
00:03:30.479 --> 00:03:36.716
A demo adds one in a hand-written loop, a million times per thread, on 1, 2, 4 and 8 threads.

68
00:03:36.716 --> 00:03:40.001
Guess the failures for one thread, and for eight.

69
00:03:43.067 --> 00:03:47.163
One thread never fails, because nobody else changes the value.

70
00:03:47.163 --> 00:03:49.739
With eight, about 2.8 million failures.

71
00:03:49.739 --> 00:03:52.580
And the counter came out exact, every time.

72
00:03:52.733 --> 00:03:55.580
2.8 million noes, and not one hurt feeling.

73
00:03:55.580 --> 00:03:58.161
A failed CAS costs a retry, not a wait.

74
00:03:58.161 --> 00:04:03.920
And it only fails because another thread succeeded, so some thread always gets through.

75
00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:05.773
That's what lock-free means.

76
00:04:06.533 --> 00:04:08.811
You'll rarely write that loop yourself.

77
00:04:08.811 --> 00:04:12.606
updateAndGet runs it for you, around a function of the old value.

78
00:04:12.606 --> 00:04:15.876
So what happens to your function when the clerk says no?

79
00:04:16.067 --> 00:04:24.439
Four threads each make 250,000 one-cent deposits with updateAndGet, and the function counts how often it runs.

80
00:04:24.439 --> 00:04:26.341
A million times, exactly?

81
00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:33.612
The balance came out at exactly a million cents.

82
00:04:33.612 --> 00:04:36.858
But the function ran 1,294,839 times.

83
00:04:36.858 --> 00:04:41.772
Every lost race ran it again, and threw the result away.

84
00:04:41.933 --> 00:04:48.520
If that function had sent the customer a text, the bank would have sent nearly 300,000 extra texts.

85
00:04:48.520 --> 00:04:53.509
So keep the function pure: it only looks at its input and returns a result.

86
00:04:54.267 --> 00:04:55.310
One last trap.

87
00:04:55.310 --> 00:04:59.407
An account holds 100, and two threads each withdraw 80.

88
00:04:59.407 --> 00:05:03.578
Each checks get, then calls addAndGet, two atomic calls.

89
00:05:03.578 --> 00:05:05.813
Can the balance go below zero?

90
00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:06.297
Yes.

91
00:05:06.297 --> 00:05:11.714
Both check, both see 100, both take 80, and the balance ends at minus 60.

92
00:05:11.714 --> 00:05:16.537
Two atomic calls in a row are two steps, with a gap between them.

93
00:05:16.733 --> 00:05:19.306
So put the check inside the request.

94
00:05:19.306 --> 00:05:25.165
Read 100, decide it covers 80, then ask the clerk: it says 100, please make it 20.

95
00:05:25.165 --> 00:05:29.809
The other thread hears no, reads 20, and sees there isn't enough.

96
00:05:30.600 --> 00:05:34.100
So how do two threads change one number without waiting?

97
00:05:34.100 --> 00:05:35.726
They never hold the board.

98
00:05:35.726 --> 00:05:41.101
They ask the clerk: write only if nothing changed, and when she says no, decide again.

99
00:05:41.101 --> 00:05:44.664
Keep the function pure, and the check inside the request.

100
00:05:44.800 --> 00:05:45.718
Quick check.

101
00:05:45.718 --> 00:05:47.094
The board says 43.

102
00:05:47.094 --> 00:05:50.840
You ask the clerk: it says 42, please make it 43.

103
00:05:50.840 --> 00:05:54.128
What does she say, and what do you do next?
